WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.540
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.110
Thank you for coming to my talk.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.990
I'll just get running
and rolling here.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.990
If you'd like to follow
along as I go through this.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.580
You can find the slides on the
conference app for this speaker

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.290
session.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.730
I also just tweeted
out the link.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.990
You can find that at markjrieke.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.930
That's i-e-k-e.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.810
But today I'll be
introducing workboots, which

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.530
is a tool and a package for
generating prediction intervals

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.970
from tidymodel workflows.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.420
So I used to be a
mechanical engineer.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.670
So before I jump into
talking about this tool,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.980
I want to talk about some other
niche and interesting tools

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.430
that I've seen as a
mechanical engineer.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.208
So this is something
interesting.

00:00:55.208 --> 00:00:56.750
If you look at it,
you might not know

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.505
what it's used for offhand.

00:00:58.505 --> 00:01:00.630
One of my friends who works
at AB sent this to me.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.520
This is a pigtail proof coil.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.420
It lets brewers take samples
from these humongous beer

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.400
tanks safely.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.370
The second tool.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.410
Again, really
interesting looking.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.880
What's it used for off the bat?

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.160
I don't know.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.130
But for folks who do know,
this is a wire crimper

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.790
and it's very useful for dealing
with large electrical lugs

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.210
again, safely and efficiently.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.502
And this abomination.

00:01:26.502 --> 00:01:28.335
We don't really have
to talk about too much.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.260
But one tool that I use
often that is again niche

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.750
and specific is this Kreg jig.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.620
On first glance, you might not
know what it would be used for.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.660
It's got this odd
cylinder on one side.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.670
But you can see that,
in a specific scenario,

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.630
it's a perfect
tool for this job.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.030
And that's kind of what I
like to think of workboots

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.320
as, it is a specific
tool for a specific job.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.210
And that specific job is for
when you are building a model

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.240
and you would like to generate
a prediction interval,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.940
but you're using a model
type that on its own

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.220
only generates
point predictions.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.500
Workboots will let
you take that model

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.800
and generate
prediction intervals.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.450
So why might you be interested
in using this package?

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.830
Well, maybe you're
somebody like me. Again,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.960
my name is Mark Rieke.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.810
I am a senior
consumer experience

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:32.450
analyst for Memorial
Hermann Health System

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.220
in Houston, Texas.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.760
We are a large hospital
system in the Houston

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.860
area with 17 hospitals, 11
of which are acute care,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.950
hundreds of clinics and
outpatient facilities

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.210
servicing about 30,000 employees,
and my small but mighty team

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.200
of four handles
and fields requests

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.740
from all 30,000 employees.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.770
So to the other team members who
are keeping the fort together,

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.490
I'm very thankful that
they let me run off to DC.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:08.690
But what we do is we deal with
our patient satisfaction survey

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:09.800
data.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.870
Our job is to administer,
understand, analyze this data

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.740
to hopefully improve our
patient satisfaction when they

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.310
go through our hospital system.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.500
And the majority of the
time, our day to day

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.980
involves answering questions.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.610
So some of the questions that
come up are ones like this.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.540
You know, we see a
pretty consistent trend

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.080
going upwards in our
patient satisfaction score

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.910
and then this drop off in April.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.820
What's going on there?

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.130
If we look at scores
across hospitals,

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.360
maybe we're seeing differences.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.420
Are these real differences
or is this just

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.210
due to sampling variation?

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.030
And thirdly, we see
different response rates

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.150
depending on if we send
invites out via email

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.310
or if we send
invites out via text.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.620
Which one should we use?

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.280
So these are all questions
that on their own,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.770
giving a point estimate for,

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:08.750
it was a good first step.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.510
But to get the full
picture, we really

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.540
need to add in uncertainty
because that will help

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.200
us separate signal from noise.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.410
So maybe we see by adding
in confidence intervals

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:24.250
that in April, perhaps there
was a disruption in the survey

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.250
distribution workflow
and we only sent out

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.460
a few surveys that month.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:32.670
So this is really just
statistical noise.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.760
If we look across hospitals,
maybe we see a lot of overlap

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.250
in the potential
underlying scores.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:44.530
So, again, nothing to be
too concerned about here.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.542
But hey, if we look at the
difference in response rates,

00:04:47.542 --> 00:04:49.250
these are pretty
significantly different.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.670
And it's readily apparent
once we add in our uncertainty

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:55.790
intervals and this is something
that we can action around.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.770
I think when I say
this out loud, we all

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.770
understand at a base level
that point estimates don't give

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.760
the full picture, but we
really need additional context

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:08.340
to help that point sink through.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.400
So this also extends
to not just when

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.840
I'm answering
specific questions,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.680
but when I'm answering
questions with a model.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.670
So what are the drivers
of patient satisfaction?

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.180
What will our score be?

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.720
How many responses
are we expecting?

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.280
These are all
questions that I can

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.080
and have answered with models.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.180
But again, because
I'm always wanting

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.450
to layer in uncertainty,
I'm really limited

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:35.190
to the types of
models that I can use.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:39.390
My problem is that this
is me, like, all the time.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.960
I, I see people using,
like, fun and fancy models,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.030
like, actually XGBoost or
like k nearest neighbors

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.090
regressors.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:47.700
But because of the
work I'm doing,

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.820
I'm really stuck
using plain old lm.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.430
So that's where workboots
comes into play.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:58.730
This is a package that hopefully
bridges the gap between simpler

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:01.220
models that let you generate
prediction intervals right out

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.100
of the bag and more
powerful models

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:09.290
that let you get non linearity
and complex interactions.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.660
Workboots hopefully gets
the best of both worlds

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.820
by letting you generate
prediction intervals

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.940
while still using your
more powerful models.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.240
So let's go into
the package itself.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.210
And I think one thing we
should do right out the gate

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.170
is look at where workboots sits
in the model building timeline.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.670
If some of you
recognize this slide,

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.820
this is something that I totally
stole from an old Julia Silge

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.820
presentation that
walks through how

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:42.030
you might go from model
conception and idea

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.430
to model finalization.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.320
So maybe you're
starting off on the left

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.530
with doing some exploratory
analysis, engineering

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:53.100
new features, and
then fitting, tuning,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.740
and evaluating a set of models.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.540
Then perhaps you
repeat this a few times

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:03.570
before you finally get
to your finalized model.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.450
A lot of this is already
handled by tidymodels.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.510
Workboots really just
comes in as that last leg

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:10.400
of the journey.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.290
So you've done all
your preprocessing,

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.690
done all your feature
engineering, all

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.220
of your hyperparameters
are tuned,

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.560
and now you're ready to
answer that big question

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.482
that your stakeholder
has been asking you.

00:07:23.482 --> 00:07:25.190
And part of the reason
why it makes sense

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.010
that workboots is
just this last step

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:31.070
is that there are a couple
of checks that you really

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.320
want to make sure you're
hitting before it makes sense

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.680
to use workboots.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.810
Again, it's a really specific
tool for a really specific job.

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:43.040
So firstly, you should make
sure that the residuals

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.090
of your model are
normally distributed.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.350
This is an assumption that
it makes under the hood.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:50.960
Secondly, you need to make sure
you actually have enough time

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.120
to run this model.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.170
I'm an analyst.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.360
I wrote this for
myself primarily.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.930
I'm never really concerned with
putting models into production.

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:03.560
So depending on the
amount of preprocessing

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.660
and the size of your
data set, workboots

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:08.750
could take anywhere
between 5 and 20 minutes

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:11.930
to output prediction intervals.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.845
So please do not
deploy this when

00:08:13.845 --> 00:08:14.970
you need to have something

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.540
answer a question in a
fraction of a second.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.070
And thirdly and finally,
most importantly, it really

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.840
makes sense to make
sure that you actually

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.390
need this more powerful
model that workboots allows

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:28.710
you to use.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.680
What I found a lot of the time
is that good old plain Jane

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.490
lm will, you know,
really get you the answers

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:39.570
that you need maybe
80, 90% of the time.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.820
But if you do find
yourself in a scenario

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.960
where you are meeting all
three of these checks,

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.290
and you're ready to
start using workboots,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:49.050
how does it actually work?

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.190
I'm going to gloss
over this very quickly.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.130
But if you'd like
to dig in deeper

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.380
into some of the math
that's underlying it,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.810
you can scan this QR
code that will take you

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:01.630
to a little bit of an explainer.
But at a very high level,

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.040
workboots takes
your training data,

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.230
generates a whole bunch
of bootstrap resamples.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:13.670
Then it fits many models
to each of these resamples

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:16.670
or excuse me, it fits a model
to each resample so that you

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.290
end up with many models.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.840
Thirdly, it takes each
of these many models

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.850
and predicts on new data.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.490
So for each observation
in your new data set,

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.650
you have many predictions.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.358
And then finally,
finally, it summarises

00:09:31.358 --> 00:09:32.900
all of these with
a prediction range.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.220
So now that we've checked
that we can use workboots,

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.910
we kind of know what's going on.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.980
Let's get into actually
using it with some code.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.020
So there's three things
that we're going to do.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.590
First of all, workboots
builds on top of tidymodels.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:54.430
So what we'll do is set
up a tidymodel workflow.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:57.850
Next, we'll use workboots
to generate our predictions.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:09:59.890
And then thirdly,
we'll use workboots

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:01.305
to summarise these results.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:08.640
There is some great
documentation on tidymodels

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.460
and how to use it,
but I will just

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.450
set up a very quick workflow
for explanation sake.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.770
So in this case, we'll be
using the Palmer penguins

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.740
data set for this example.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.320
This is a data set that
contains a bunch of information

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.600
and characteristics
about penguins

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.130
from different
islands, such as their

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:34.620
bill length, bill depth,
what species they are,

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:36.750
which island they
came from, and what

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:38.405
we'll be predicting
on their body mass.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.060
As is best practice,

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.450
for this example, we'll split
into a testing and training

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:44.995
data set.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:51.940
So next we'll set up a pretty
basic preprocessing recipe.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:55.360
In this case, again, we'll be
predicting each penguin's body

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.580
mass based on all
of the data that's

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.810
available in the data set.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.120
And then we'll combine
this preprocessing

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.430
with a model in a workflow.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.910
In this case, using
a boosted tree model

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.830
for regression,
which uses XGBoost

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.320
under the hood. As
a reminder, XGBoost

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.400
can only generate point
predictions on its own.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.073
So now that we have
the workflow set up,

00:11:20.073 --> 00:11:21.240
we're done with tidymodels.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:22.573
Now we can jump into workboots.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.950
One of the large workhorse
functions within the package

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:31.860
is this predict boot's function.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.810
This is going to take the
workflow that we just set up.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.240
We'll pass that
to predict boots.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:46.280
Specify the number of bootstrap
resamples that we'd like to take.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:50.060
And then because we are fitting
and predicting all in one

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.880
fell swoop, we're going
to pass both the training

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.800
data and the new data that
we're going to predict on.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:56.400
In this case,

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.060
this is the prenguins
test data set.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:05.580
And what this returns is a
tibble with nested predictions

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.990
for each observation
in the test data set.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.520
So in this case, each row is
a penguin in penguins test

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.520
and we have 2000
estimations of their weight

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.750
from our many models
that we created.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.210
We can summarise this by
passing this to the summarise

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:21.590
predictions function.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:26.750
This just returns a median, lower,
and upper prediction interval

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.560
range.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.840
And just like
that, we have taken

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.060
a model that only
generates point predictions

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:37.450
and returned prediction ranges,
excuse me, prediction intervals.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.150
By default workboots
and predict boots

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:46.730
will generate
prediction intervals.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:49.520
But if we so desire,
we can change

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.580
this to generate confidence
intervals by setting interval

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:54.560
to confidence.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.180
And just as a reminder of the
difference between the two,

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.910
here's an example, looking
at the Ames housing data set.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:05.780
In this case, this example is
predicting each home's sale

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:08.330
price based on the first
floor square footage.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:09.920
On the left,
prediction intervals

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:13.340
are the range in which
we might expect to find

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.250
any individual home's price.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.860
And on the right is an example
of a confidence interval, which

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.402
is the range in
which we might expect

00:13:20.402 --> 00:13:21.595
to find the model output.

00:13:26.365 --> 00:13:27.740
Thirdly, and
finally, in addition

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:30.710
to generating prediction
and confidence intervals,

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.500
workboots allows us
to generate estimations

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.510
of variable importance.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:40.830
This function that does this is
very similar to predict boots.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:45.870
It takes the workflow that we've
created, passes it to vi boots.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:48.510
We specify again the
number of resamples

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:53.680
that we'd like to take and
give it our training data.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:58.570
And what this returns is a
tibble with nested estimations

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:04.100
of variable importance for
each feature in the model.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:07.410
And what we can see
from this example.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:09.480
Oh, sorry, missed a slide.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.730
And again, there's another
summarise function

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:17.070
that lets us return a median,
lower, and upper interval range.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:21.820
We can pass this to summarise
importance to get this output.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:25.900
And what we can see from this
example is that flipper length

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.620
and whether this species
was of the Gentoo species

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:32.620
is pretty important
to this XGBoost

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.560
or this set of XGBoost
models in determining

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:37.210
each penguin's weight.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.670
But there are wide ranges
around those estimations

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:40.870
of importance.

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:44.860
And the other hand, what
island the penguin comes from

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.100
is, we're pretty confident

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.830
it's not very important.

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:52.100
So in closing, my
call to action for you

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.460
all is to give
workboots, a shot.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:58.370
Again, I think this is
a very specific tool

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.770
for a very specific job.

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.530
But if you find yourself
in the situation

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:06.830
where you need to generate
prediction intervals

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.230
and you don't want to be
limited by model type,

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:11.810
I think workboots might
be the package for you.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.380
So thank you.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:16.170
I'm happy to take any questions.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.520
And I have some links for
additional reading material

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.310
that that'll if you're
